## Category B: Incident Management (From Detection to Resolution)

**5. Incidents need a timeline visualization, not just a table.**
Lines 464+ show incidents as a table with KPIs (count, high severity, unrouted). But incidents have a lifecycle: detected → declared → mitigated → resolved. Show a Gantt-style timeline of open incidents with their duration bars, so the DBA sees "this one has been open for 3 days" at a glance.

*Reasoning:* Duration is invisible in a table (you have to mentally subtract DETECTED_AT from now). A timeline makes long-lived incidents visually prominent — they're wider bars.

**6. Incident proposals should show "evidence quality" — why this grouping makes sense.**
Lines 67-105 generate incident declarations from proposal keys (rule family + entity). But the proposal view doesn't explain WHY these alerts belong together. Show: "3 alerts in the COST family on WH_ALFA_TRANSFORM_PRD within 2 hours" — the temporal and entity clustering logic should be visible.

*Reasoning:* A DBA won't declare an incident they don't understand. Showing the grouping evidence builds confidence to click "Declare."

**7. Add "incident reopen detection" — when a resolved incident's condition recurs.**
Line 109: close is forward-only; reopening creates a new incident with `REOPENED_FROM`. But there's no automatic detection of "the alert condition that caused this incident just fired again." The DBA should see a banner: "Similar to resolved incident INC-0047 (3 days ago)."

*Reasoning:* Recurring incidents are the strongest signal that a fix didn't work. Automatic correlation between new alerts and recently-resolved incidents is what makes incident management proactive rather than reactive.

**8. The incident close workflow should capture "time-to-mitigate" separately from "time-to-resolve."**
Line 108-121: close writes `RESOLVED_AT`. But mitigation (bleeding stopped) and resolution (root cause fixed) are different timestamps. A DBA who hot-fixes at 2am resolves the symptom; the engineering fix lands days later. Track both for honest MTTR reporting.

*Reasoning:* MTTR (mean time to resolve) is meaningless if it blends "we stopped the bleeding in 10 minutes" with "we shipped the permanent fix 3 weeks later." Separate TTM (time to mitigate) from TTR gives leadership honest operational metrics.

